# build output
bin/
*.prg
*.iq
*.debug.xml

# local SDK state
*.log

# python
__pycache__/

# Recorded activities. A FIT file carries the watch's serial number, a full
# heart rate trace and the exact time every game was played, so these stay out
# of a public repo. Keep them somewhere local and point the tools at that.
*.fit
Past Data/

# Everything pulled off the watch, not just the FIT files. The debug and
# Connect IQ logs that come with them are device state too, and the directory
# is a working area rather than something the repo should carry.
data/

# Local virtualenv for reading FIT files.
.venv-fit/

# Secrets and local environment. None of these has ever been committed; these
# lines exist so that an accidental `git add -A` cannot be the first time.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Signing material. The Connect IQ developer key must never enter a public
# repo: Garmin does not retain a copy, so whoever holds it is the only one
# who can ship an update to the listing - and anyone who obtains it can ship
# one as you. Garmin staff on the record: "We do not retain your key - your
# local copy, and any backups you may make, are all that exist."
*.der
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

# Python bytecode outside a __pycache__ directory. The one sensitive-class
# file ever committed to this repo was a .pyc, which embedded a local
# filesystem path; __pycache__/ was ignored afterwards but a stray .pyc
# beside a script still was not.
*.pyc
*.pyo

# Watch-side state pulled off the device by tools/watch.sh. These carry the
# unit_id, which is the watch's serial number in plain text.
*.SET
err_log.txt
err_log.bak
CIQ_LOG.*
